BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Eskenazi School of Art, Architecture + Design
DTSTART:20260722T150000Z
DTEND:20260722T200000Z
LOCATION:McCalla Building\, 525 E. 9th Street
SUMMARY:McCalla Open Gallery Hours
DESCRIPTION:Outfitting IU Athletics: Hoosier Champions                  T
 his exhibit showcases objects from IU's championship teams and athletes\,
  connecting them to historic achievements in the Big Ten and the national
  stage. The items featured in the exhibit are from the IU Athletics Sport
 s Memorabilia Collection and feature photos and videos provided by IU Ath
 letics and Indiana University Archives.                Dates: November 14
 \, 2025 - October 2026          &#160\;                  &#160\;        B
 y Their Own Hands: The Black Filmmakers Hall of Fame!          Founded in
  1974 by the Culture and Ethnic Affairs Guild of the Oakland Museum of Ca
 lifornia\, the Black Filmmakers Hall of Fame began as a film lecture seri
 es and awards ceremony celebrating achievements of Black film industry fi
 gures. When that organization closed\, they donated their materials to IU
 . In collaboration with the IU Black Film Center and Archive\, this exhib
 it features objects from that collection\, including founding documents\,
  costumes\, and handprints of inductees to the Hall of Fame.        Dates
 : September 2025 - May 2026          &#160\;                  &#160\;    
     Capturing the Campus: T.C. Steele\, Indiana University's First Artist
  in ResidenceCapturing the Campus pays tribute to renowned Hoosier artist
  T.C. Steele. The exhibit honors the century that has passed since his de
 ath by showcasing paintings form the artist's residency at IU and other r
 elevant works. The exhibit will bring together Steele's landscapes from t
 he IU Public Art and Cultural Heritage Collection and the Indiana State M
 useum and Historic Sites' collection\, allowing the holdings of these two
  collections to be viewed in one space.        Dates: April 17\, 2026 - A
 pril 2026          &#160\;                  &#160\;        A Stitch in Ti
 me: Home Sewing of the 1960s and 1970s.          Take a look back in time
  to the home-sewn fashions of the 1960s and 1970s in the exhibit A Stitch
  in Time: Home Sewing of the 1960s and 1970s. The garments on view are fr
 om IU's own Elizabeth Sage Historic Costume collection\, known for their 
 Glenn Close Costume Collection\, haute couture\, and Hoosier native desig
 ners like Bill Blass\, Halston\, and Norman Norell.        This exhibit i
 s curated by Beth Battles for her capstone project for her Curatorship MA
  program.Dates: April 24\, 2026-July 24\, 2026          &#160\;        Th
 e Ethics of Curation: Student-Curated Mulitmedia Exhibition          This
  collaborative student-curated exhibit examines curation and its ethical 
 dimensions. The exhibit reflects upon and remembers the past while workin
 g to make museums and curation more inclusive in the future. Objects feat
 ured include paintings\, photography\, and dioramas. Students curated thi
 s exhibit as part of the class SP26-A344 Curatorial Ethics taught by Dr. 
 Faye R. Gleisser.        Dates: April 24\, 2026-July 20\, 2026Multipurpos
 e Room          Currently Featuring: The Gift of Collecting: Spotlight on
  Rudy Pozzatti          Rudy Pozzatti\, a world-renowned artist\, is attr
 ibuted with developing the printmaking program at Indiana University. Thi
 s exhibit features his works\, which were donated by Jim and Joyce Grando
 rf to the IU Public Art and Cultural Heritage Collection.                
   &#160\;        Media Gallery          Currently Featuring: Protesting S
 outh African Apartheid: A Visual Record          During the 1970s and 198
 0s\, the global movement against apartheid—the totalizing system of whi
 te supremacy that prevailed in South Africa—took on new energy and broa
 der dimensions\, both inside and outside the country.                  Ea
 sy to reproduce and circulate\, eye-catching posters provided an excellen
 t medium for spreading the anti-apartheid message\, especially in the tow
 nships. Still\, doing so risked attention from the security police and po
 tential arrest. Until the internal mass democratic movement of the 1980s\
 , many posters were printed and circulated in exile.                  Thi
 s exhibit provides a look at poster designs from this pivotal era of Sout
 h African History. The posters\, all drawn from IU's African Studies Coll
 ection at the Wells Library\, have been selected and curated by the stude
 nts in professor Alex Lichtenstein's course\, Spring 2026 HON-H251\, "His
 tory and Heritage in South Africa."          &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<table style="border-collapse: collapse\; wi
 dth: 97.3422%\;" border="1"><tbody><tr><td style="width: 99.9123%\;" cols
 pan="2"><table style="border-collapse: collapse\; width: 97.3422%\;" bord
 er="1"><tbody><tr><td style="width: 99.9123%\;"><p style="text-align: cen
 ter\;"><strong><em>Outfitting IU Athletics: Hoosier Champions</em></stron
 g></p><p style="text-align: left\;">                  This exhibit showca
 ses objects from IU's championship teams and athletes\, connecting them t
 o historic achievements in the Big Ten and the national stage. The items 
 featured in the exhibit are from the IU Athletics Sports Memorabilia Coll
 ection and feature photos and videos provided by IU Athletics and Indiana
  University Archives.                </p><p style="text-align: center\;">
 <strong>Dates: November 14\, 2025 - October 2026</strong></p><strong><pic
 ture class="lw_image lw_image29062  lw_align_left"><source media="(max-wi
