 >          &#160\;        </p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="2"><p style="tex
 t-align: center\;"><em><strong>Media Gallery</strong></em></p><p>        
   Currently Featuring:<i> Protesting South African Apartheid: A Visual Re
 cord</i></p><p>          During the 1970s and 1980s\, the global movement
  against apartheid—the totalizing system of white supremacy that prevai
 led in South Africa—took on new energy and broader dimensions\, both in
 side and outside the country.        </p><p>          Easy to reproduce a
 nd circulate\, eye-catching posters provided an excellent medium for spre
 ading the anti-apartheid message\, especially in the townships. Still\, d
 oing so risked attention from the security police and potential arrest. U
 ntil the internal mass democratic movement of the 1980s\, many posters we
 re printed and circulated in exile.        </p><p>          This exhibit 
 provides a look at poster designs from this pivotal era of South African 
 History. The posters\, all drawn from IU's African Studies Collection at 
 the Wells Library\, have been selected and curated by the students in pro
 fessor Alex Lichtenstein's course\, Spring 2026 HON-H251\, "History and H
 eritage in South Africa."        </p><p><picture class="lw_image lw_image
 31100  lw_align_left"><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type="image/webp
 " srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/479/width/500/hei
